    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures). Here's what I changed to satisfy the specification.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `householdId` is now a deterministic function of the household key `(normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode)` \u2014 first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 \u2014 and everything that keys off the household uses it.\n\n**`AssignHouseholdId.java`**\n- `householdId(lastName, postcode)` now hashes `normalize(lastName) + \"|\" + postcode` (was `lastName + \"|\" + normalizedAddress`).\n- `service` now **always** assigns the id (no longer gated on `sharesHousehold`), so owners with the same lastName+postcode share it automatically. Added a `forRequest(...)` helper that returns the id, or `null` when no postcode is supplied (no household).\n\n**`OwnerIdentityKey.java`**\n- `forRequest` now derives the household component via `AssignHouseholdId.forRequest`, so the consolidated identityKey always reflects the computed household rather than only when `sharesHousehold` was set.\n\n**`RequireUniqueIdentity.java`**\n- Added the household-duplicate block: if an existing owner shares the new owner's computed `householdId`, the create is rejected **409** \u2014 **unless** the request sets `sharesHousehold`, in which case it's admitted as a declared household member. The exact whole-identityKey check remains (a real duplicate person is still rejected even with `sharesHousehold`).\n\n**`AssignPossibleDuplicate.java`**\n- Now matches on the computed `householdId` (instead of re-deriving lastName+postcode) and returns early when `sharesHousehold` is set \u2014 a declared member is not a suspected duplicate, so `possibleDuplicate` stays false. Dropped the now-unused `normalize`/`Locale`.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `householdId` and `sharesHousehold` descriptions so the API docs (and generated DTO javadoc) match the new behaviour.\n\nThe one behaviour I had to decide (spec didn't state it): an owner with **no postcode** has no household \u2014 `householdId` is left `null` and it never participates in household duplicate/soft-duplicate matching. This preserves the existing \"no postcode never matches\" convention and keeps all no-postcode owners (the common `ownerNode()` payload) working as before.",
